Aim
To develop practical Python programming skills by typing, saving, running and debugging programs using IDLE and Google Colab.

1. Tools Used
IDLE: Python software installed on the lab computer. It works offline and saves .py files.
Google Colab: Cloud-based Python platform that runs programs through a browser.
GitHub: Used to store and manage Python programs and maintain a record of completed laboratory work.

2. Five Phases of the Lab
Tooling: Set up IDLE, Google Colab and GitHub.
Operations: Type, save and run Python programs.
Inspection: Test programs using incorrect inputs and boundary values.
Dispatch: Commit and upload completed work to GitHub.
Teach-Back: Demonstrate the working program to another pair.

3. Commissioning Checklist
Open IDLE and test basic Python calculations.
Open Google Colab and run a basic Python statement.
Sign in to GitHub and access the laboratory repository.
Maintain the lab record with date, work order and partner details.
Keep the handwritten program ready for typing and testing.

4. Operations – Python Programming Tasks

IT12 – Hello, Shop Floor
Create a simple greeting program.
Take the operator's name as input.
Display a personalized greeting.
Run the program in both IDLE and Google Colab.

IT10 – The Batch Inspector
Read 10 machine-part diameter values.
Convert inputs into floating-point numbers.
Classify parts into ACCEPT, REWORK and SCRAP.
Maintain three separate counters.
Display the final inspection report.

IT8 – The Pallet Clerk
Take total parts and box size as input.
Calculate full boxes using floor division.
Calculate remaining parts using modulus.
Verify the total parts using an audit statement.

IT6 – End of Shift
Perform batch inspection.
Pack only accepted parts into boxes of 4.
Calculate full boxes and loose parts.
Display all three quality counts in the final report.

5. Inspection – Error Testing
Important errors and tests performed:
ValueError: Occurs when text such as "twentyfive" is entered instead of a numerical diameter.
Boundary Testing: Check exact values 24.95 and 25.05 to verify correct classification.
Silent Audit: Verify that the packing calculation produces the correct result.
Blind Read: Compare the expected output with the actual program output.
Important Learning
TypeError occurs when incompatible data types are used.
ValueError occurs when a value cannot be converted into the required type.
Boundary conditions must be tested carefully.
Every defect should be recorded with its description, cause and correction.

